.TH FORTRAN 1 17-Dec-83
.SH NAME
fortran \- SVS FORTRAN compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fortran 
[
.B \-o
ofile ] [
.B \-i
] [
.B \-c
] [
.B \-u
] [
.B \-v
] [
.B \-f
] [
.B \-d
] [
.B \-m
] file ... 
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fortran\^ ,
the FORTRAN compiler, accepts a list of FORTRAN source files
and various intermediate texts contained in the list of files specified by
.I file\^
and puts the resulting executable object module in
.I a.out\^
(but see the
.B \-o
option, described below).
.PP
In order to understand the use of
.I fortran\^ ,
the reader should be aware of the
steps through which the compiler goes in order to turn a FORTRAN source
program into an executable object file.
.PP
The FORTRAN compiler generates several intermediate files on the way to
generating the final executable file.
The first phase of the compiler generates an intermediate file, of the same
name as the source file, but with a \fI.i\^\fR suffix.
This intermediate file is destined for processing by the code generator.
.PP
The code generator is the second phase of the process.
The output of the code generator is a file with the same name as the source
file, but with a suffix of \fI.obj\^\fR.
The \(**\fI.obj\^\fR file is the input to the next phase, called 
\fIulinker\fR.
.PP
The \fIulinker\fR phase of the compilation process performs two
functions:  it links the \fI.obj\^\fR files, resolving external references
to the FORTRAN-77 run-time library; then it converts the \fI.obj\^\fR
file format into a \s-1UNIX\s+1-style object file, with a \fI.o\fR suffix.  
This means that one file in the list of files specified on the
.I fortran\^
command line \fImust\fR contain a main program.  This \fI.o\fR file
can then be processed by the \s-1UNIX\s+1 system loader utility, 
\fIld\^\fR.
.PP
Finally, the \fIld\^\fR utility produces the final executable code file.
.PP
When using
.I fortran\^ ,
any combination of FORTRAN source files (each having a \fI.for\fR suffix)
can be combined with
FORTRAN or Pascal intermediate files (each having
a \fI.i\^\fR suffix), 
FORTRAN or Pascal object code files (each having a \fI.obj\^\fR suffix),
and \s-1UNIX\s+1 object files (each having a \fI.o\fR suffix).
When the compilation completes successfully, the result of the combination of
all those files is placed in the file 
.I a.out\^
or in the file specified by the
.B \-o
option.
.PP
The following is a list of options to \fIfortran\^\fR:
.IP \fB\-o\fR
The
.B \-o
option, if used, specifies that the file 
.I ofile\^
(runnable file) whose name follows the option is the file to receive the final
executable code.  If the
.B \-o
option is not specified, the resultant executable
code is placed in the file
.I a.out\^ .
.IP \fB\-i\fR
If the
.B \-i
option is used, the FORTRAN intermediate code (the result of running
\fI/usr/lib/fortran\^\fR)
is placed in a file of the same name as the source file, but with a suffix
of \fI.i\^\fR appended.  The compilation then terminates.
.IP \fB\-c\fR
If the
.B \-c
option is used, the FORTRAN unlinked object code (the result of
running
\fI/usr/lib/code\^\fR)
is placed in a file of the same name as the source file, but with a 
suffix of \fI.obj\fR appended.  The compilation then terminates.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
If the
.B \-u
option is used, the linked object code (the result of running
\fI/usr/lib/ulinker\^\fR)
is placed in a file of the same name as the source file, but with a 
suffix of \fI.o\fR appended.  The compilation then terminates.
.IP \fB\-v\fR
The
.B \-v
option (for verbose) makes
.I fortran\^
display a running progress report as
it compiles.
If the
.B \-v
option is specified, the compiler also generates
a listing file, of the same name as the source file,
but with a suffix of \fI.lst\^\fR appended.
.IP \fB\-m\fR
If the
.B \-m
option is specified, the link map information
from executing
.I /usr/lib/ulinker\^
will be saved in a file,
of the same name as the source file, but with a suffix
of \fI.map\^\fR appended.
.IP \fB\-f\fR
If the
.B \-f
option is specified,
the library
.I /usr/lib/sky.paslib.o\^
will be used instead of
.I /usr/lib/paslib.o\^ .
This is the ``floating point'' option.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
If the
.B \-d
option is specified,
information for the symbolic debugger (see \fIdbg\fR)
will be output to the object file.
.PP
If only one 
.I file\^
argument is supplied on the command line, then all the intermediate files
(\fI.i, .obj, .o\^\fR) are removed at the end of the compilation.
If multiple 
.I file\^
arguments are typed on the command line, any existing intermediate files are not
removed.
.PP
A special archiver,
.I /usr/bin/library\^ ,
prompts for FORTRAN object names
(\fI.obj\fI\^) as input, and creates a FORTRAN library consisting of those objects.
Libraries created by this archiver are usable only
for FORTRAN or PASCAL compilations, and not compatible with
any other \s-1UNIX\s+1 archives.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
fortran prog1.for
.PP
compiles ``prog1.for''
and puts the resulting object module
in 
``a.out''.
.IP
fortran -o frammis prog2.for prog3.obj
.PP
compiles the FORTRAN program called 
``prog2.for''
and links the result with the object file
``prog3.obj''.
The result of the compilation is placed in the output file called
``frammis''.
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/usr/lib/sky.paslib.obj      'u
.nf
\(**.for	FORTRAN source
\(**.i	Intermediate code
\(**.obj	Compiled but unlinked \fBfortran\fR object-code
\(**.o	Compiled but unlinked \s-1UNIX\s+1 object-code
/usr/lib/ftnlib.obj	FORTRAN run-time library
/usr/lib/paslib.obj	Pascal run-time library (software FP)
/usr/lib/sky.paslib.obj	Pascal run-time library (hardware FP)
/usr/lib/fortran	FORTRAN compiler
/usr/lib/code	Code generator
/usr/lib/dbg	Symbolic debugger executable
/usr/lib/ulinker	SVS to \s-1UNIX\s+1 object-file converter
/usr/lib/ftncterrs	FORTRAN compile-time error message file
/usr/lib/ftnrterrs	FORTRAN run-time error message file
/bin/ld	linking loader
/lib/crt0.o	startup routine
/usr/bin/library	Interactive FORTRAN/Pascal .obj archiver
.fi
.DT
